WEBVTT

1
00:00:11.200 --> 00:00:19.446
"We must rest," Long John said, collapsing on the ground when we finally got to the top of the plateau.

2
00:00:19.447 --> 00:00:25.075
The land descended toward the west, and we all sat and admired the view around us.

3
00:00:25.076 --> 00:00:31.585
Not a sail was upon the sea, and the vastness of the view increased our sense of solitude.

4
00:00:31.586 --> 00:00:38.742
The only sound was the distant surf from Cape of the Woods and the nearby chirp of insects.

5
00:00:38.743 --> 00:00:44.820
"Nothing stops the sea," Dick said. "It makes me feel very alone."

6
00:00:44.821 --> 00:00:47.250
Long John took bearings with his compass.

7
00:00:47.251 --> 00:00:53.276
"There are three 'tall trees' in a direct line from Skeleton Island.

8
00:00:53.277 --> 00:00:57.544
'Spyglass shoulder' means that lower point there.

9
00:00:57.545 --> 00:01:00.808
It's simple to find the treasure now."

10
00:01:00.809 --> 00:01:08.079
"I don't feel so good," Morgan growled. "Thinking of Flint has upset me."

11
00:01:08.080 --> 00:01:13.440
"Just be thankful he's dead," said Long John in a low voice.

12
00:01:13.441 --> 00:01:20.755
"He was an ugly devil," another pirate whispered with a shudder, "and his face was blue!"

13
00:01:20.756 --> 00:01:25.078
"The rum did that," George Merry whispered back.

14
00:01:25.079 --> 00:01:31.590
All of a sudden, a thin, high, trembling voice struck up a well-known tune:

15
00:01:31.591 --> 00:01:40.267
"Fifteen men on a dead man's chest—Yo-ho-ho, and a bottle of rum!"

16
00:01:40.268 --> 00:01:43.489
The song stopped as suddenly as it began.

17
00:01:43.490 --> 00:01:47.786
Instantly, the color drained from every pirate's face.

18
00:01:47.787 --> 00:01:53.958
Two leaped to their feet, and the man with the bandaged head grabbed at the pirate next to him in fear.

19
00:01:53.959 --> 00:01:59.009
Tom Morgan crawled on the ground, trying to find a place to hide.

20
00:01:59.010 --> 00:02:04.568
"It's Flint!" George Merry cried. "His ghost walks!"

21
00:02:04.569 --> 00:02:12.187
"Come now, this won't do," croaked Long John, struggling to get the words out with ashen lips.

22
00:02:12.188 --> 00:02:23.225
"I don't recognize the voice, but someone's trying to scare us—someone who's flesh and blood."

23
00:02:23.226 --> 00:02:28.834
Long John's courage came back as he spoke, and the others nodded, less fearful now.

24
00:02:28.835 --> 00:02:37.999
But then the same voice broke out again—not in song, but in a faint call that echoed among the cliffs of Spyglass Hill.

25
00:02:38.000 --> 00:02:45.404
"Darby McGraw, Darby McGraw!" it wailed again and again.

26
00:02:45.405 --> 00:02:53.103
Then, rising a little higher, it said, "Fetch the rum, Darby!"

27
00:02:53.104 --> 00:02:56.987
The pirates remained rooted to the ground, their eyes bulging.

28
00:02:56.988 --> 00:03:01.699
Long after the voice had died away, they stared in silence.

29
00:03:01.700 --> 00:03:06.941
"That does it!" one gasped. "Let's get out of here."

30
00:03:06.942 --> 00:03:10.369
"Those were his last words," Tom Morgan moaned.

31
00:03:10.370 --> 00:03:16.243
"Flint died asking for more rum. Nobody but us knows that!"

32
00:03:16.244 --> 00:03:22.304
Dick took his Bible out and knelt. "Our Father," he started to pray.

33
00:03:22.305 --> 00:03:26.444
Long John was shivering, but he wasn't about to surrender.

34
00:03:26.445 --> 00:03:34.385
"Flint never scared me in his life, and by thunder, he doesn't scare me now that he's dead.

35
00:03:34.386 --> 00:03:42.775
There's seven hundred thousand dollars worth of gold not a quarter of a mile from here.

36
00:03:42.776 --> 00:03:45.809
Who's going to turn his back on that?

37
00:03:45.810 --> 00:03:53.799
Flint was a drunken, old seaman with a blue face—and he's dead!"

38
00:03:53.800 --> 00:04:01.314
"Stop talking like that, Long John!" George Merry hissed. "Don't insult a ghost."

39
00:04:01.315 --> 00:04:05.609
They huddled around Long John, as if his courage could protect them.

40
00:04:05.610 --> 00:04:20.484
"Ghost? Well, maybe," answered Long John. "But I heard an echo too. No ghost has a shadow. So what's he doing with an echo?"

41
00:04:20.485 --> 00:04:26.562
I thought it was a weak argument, but one can never tell what superstitious people will believe.

42
00:04:26.563 --> 00:04:31.444
However, George Merry was greatly relieved. "You're right, Long John!

43
00:04:31.445 --> 00:04:37.975
Come to think, it was like Flint's voice, but not as clear. It was like—"

44
00:04:37.976 --> 00:04:43.530
"By thunder—Ben Gunn!" roared Long John.

45
00:04:43.531 --> 00:04:49.665
"Aye," shouted Tom Morgan. "It was Ben Gunn!"

46
00:04:49.666 --> 00:04:55.524
"What?" said Dick. "Ben Gunn's not here any more than Flint is."

47
00:04:55.525 --> 00:05:00.408
"Ah, Dick, who's to say he's not?" George Merry cried.

48
00:05:00.409 --> 00:05:05.997
"He was left here, but he still might be alive after all this time."

49
00:05:05.998 --> 00:05:09.638
"That's right!" agreed the man with the head wound.

50
00:05:09.639 --> 00:05:15.051
Their spirits soared, and the pirates grabbed their tools and started off again.

51
00:05:15.052 --> 00:05:19.307
George Merry led with the compass to keep them on the right course.

52
00:05:19.308 --> 00:05:25.400
But Dick, who was very scared and feeling more ill from his malaria, still held his Bible.

53
00:05:25.401 --> 00:05:33.074
"You spoiled your Bible. It can't protect you now," said Long John with a sly grin.

54
00:05:33.075 --> 00:05:38.408
All of us started walking downhill through the pines, great and small.

55
00:05:38.409 --> 00:05:44.355
"Two of these tall trees are wrong by the compass bearings," Long John said.

56
00:05:44.356 --> 00:05:50.390
"But that third tree is tall enough, almost two hundred feet.

57
00:05:50.391 --> 00:05:55.452
It must have been a landmark for sailors out at sea."

58
00:05:55.453 --> 00:05:59.371
The thought of the treasure erased their fear and they started to run.

59
00:05:59.372 --> 00:06:04.303
A whole lifetime of riches and pleasure lay waiting for them.

60
00:06:04.304 --> 00:06:13.807
Long John hobbled on his crutch. His nostrils quivered, and he cursed like a madman as he pulled my leash. "Come on, boy!"

61
00:06:13.808 --> 00:06:16.633
"He's not even pretending," I said to myself.

62
00:06:16.634 --> 00:06:23.197
"He'll seize the treasure, find the Hispaniola, cut every honest throat, and sail away."

63
00:06:23.198 --> 00:06:28.455
I was too haunted by the human tragedy that had happened here to keep up with the pirates.

64
00:06:28.456 --> 00:06:33.048
"How this peaceful place must have echoed with screams!" I thought.

65
00:06:33.049 --> 00:06:37.484
Long John jerked the rope again and gave me another murderous look.

66
00:06:37.485 --> 00:06:42.059
Dick dropped farther behind as his fever got worse.

67
00:06:42.060 --> 00:06:45.010
We were now at the edge of the woods.

68
00:06:45.011 --> 00:06:49.380
"Hurry, mates! Run!" George Merry shouted up ahead.

69
00:06:49.381 --> 00:06:55.506
And suddenly, not ten yards further, the men stopped and a low cry arose.

70
00:06:55.507 --> 00:07:00.416
Long John doubled his pace, pounding through the dirt with his crutch until finally

71
00:07:00.417 --> 00:07:06.248
he and I came to a dead halt as we joined the others before a huge hole.

72
00:07:06.249 --> 00:07:12.279
"It's old," I thought. "The sides have fallen in, and grass is growing on the bottom."

73
00:07:12.280 --> 00:07:16.701
Then I saw a broken pick and the boards of several packing cases.

74
00:07:16.702 --> 00:07:21.390
One board was branded "Walrus"—the name of Flint's ship.

75
00:07:21.391 --> 00:07:30.578
George Merry choked. "Someone beat us to it. The treasure's gone!"
